---
import Adr from "../components/Adr.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import Excerpt from "../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Inferred from "../components/Inferred.astro";
import Layer from "../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Path from "../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../components/Sequence.astro";

const REQ = "server/src/iki_server/entities/requests.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const MODELS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/models.py";
const LIMS = "plugins/example_lims/types";

const boxes = [
  { id: "registry", x: 20, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Registry", sub: "identities; registrars" },
  { id: "asking", x: 250, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Asking project", sub: "owns the request" },
  { id: "intake", x: 480, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Intake", sub: "types and sources" },
  { id: "service", x: 710, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Service project", sub: "serves it" },
  { id: "entries", x: 20, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Entries and records", sub: "registry XOR project" },
  { id: "submit", x: 250, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Submit", sub: "one service, for good", strong: true },
  { id: "links", x: 480, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Project links", sub: "via_request" },
  { id: "guard", x: 710, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Side guard", sub: "iki.request_side" },
  { id: "share", x: 20, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "shareable_into", sub: "one sharing check" },
  { id: "result", x: 250, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Result", sub: "made in the service" },
  { id: "release", x: 480, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Release", sub: "signed; then final", strong: true },
  { id: "told", x: 710, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Audit and notices", sub: "both sides told" },
];
const edges = [
  { from: "registry", to: "asking" },
  { from: "asking", to: "submit" },
  { from: "intake", to: "service" },
  { from: "submit", to: "intake", label: "must match" },
  { from: "submit", to: "links" },
  { from: "registry", to: "entries" },
  { from: "entries", to: "submit" },
  { from: "service", to: "guard", label: "writes its fields" },
  { from: "share", to: "submit", label: "checks" },
  { from: "result", to: "release" },
  { from: "release", to: "links", label: "back to the asker" },
  { from: "release", to: "told" },
];

const H = {
  short: "What a request is",
  how: "From an asking project's request to a released result",
  example: "Long-read sequencing from a core facility, under Regulated QC",
  rules: "Intake, sides, sending, release and what each project sees",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "flow", label: "One request, from asking to release" },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A request is a record in the project that asks, sent once to another project that serves it.
      Both sides write the same record: the asker its own fields, the service the fields the plugin
      gave it, and a database trigger keeps each to its side. Sending links the request and the
      records it names into the service. The service makes results in its own project, and a result
      reaches the asking project only when it's released, usually by a signed state. Released, it's
      final; a correction before release sends it back for review.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A request is owned by the project that asks, and served by another (<Adr n="0132" />).</li>
      <li>A result leaves its service only by release (<Adr n="0149" />).</li>
      <li>A corrected result goes back for review (<Adr n="0179" />).</li>
      <li>Identities live in registries; each project keeps its own records (<Adr n="0115" />).</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      A request type is an ordinary plugin type with a <code>request:</code> block; a result type
      has a <code>result:</code> block naming the request field and the released states. The core
      adds a <code>Request</code> row, the service's intake, and the links that carry records
      across.
    </p>
    <Diagram id="reqs" title="Projects, a request between them, and its released result" width={920} height={350} boxes={boxes} edges={edges}>
      <div slot="registry">
        <p>
          Every project is in one registry, fixed once it has entities. A registry is where an
          identity is registered once; registrars curate its facts (<Adr n={[115, 143]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/models.py" sym="class Registry(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/registries.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="asking">
        <p>
          The asking project owns the request: its Editors fill it in and send it. A project that doesn't allow sharing can't send one (<Adr n={[67, 132]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/models.py" sym="allow_sharing = models.BooleanField(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="intake">
        <p>
          A service's Admins say which request types it takes, and from whom: the whole registry, or
          named projects. A project can't name itself (<Adr n="0132" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={MODELS} sym="class Intake(" />, <Path p={MODELS} sym='name="iki_intake_not_itself"' />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def set_intake(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="service">
        <p>
          The project that serves requests. Its role holders are told when one arrives, and see the
          request and what it sends, but nothing else of the asker's (<Adr n="0142" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/declared.py" sym="def sent(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="entries">
        <p>
          Every entity is one row: an entry has a registry and no project; a record has a project.
          The database holds that, and that a project has one record per entry (<Adr n="0115" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={MODELS} sym='name="iki_entity_entry_or_record"' />, <Path p={MODELS} sym='name="iki_entity_record_once"' /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="submit">
        <p>
          Sending locks the request and checks: an Editor of the asking project, the current
          revision, not already sent, a service whose intake accepts it, sharing allowed, and every
          record it sends shareable into the service. Then it makes the <code>Request</code> row,
          links, audits both sides and tells the service (<Adr n={[132, 142]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={REQ} sym="def submit(" />, <Path p={MODELS} sym="class Request(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="links">
        <p>
          A project link lists the projects an entity is in. Links a request made carry <code
            >via_request</code
          >: the request in its service, a record it sends, and a result released back. Those
          links can't be removed while they're needed (<Adr n="0132" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={MODELS} sym="class ProjectLink(" />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def why_kept(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="guard">
        <p>
          A trigger on each request type's tables refuses an asker changing service columns, and a
          service changing the rest or inserting or deleting rows. The server says which side is
          writing in the session setting <code>iki.request_side</code> (<Adr n={[120, 132]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/migrations/0015_requests.py" sym="iki_request_guard()" />, <Path p={REQ} sym='SIDE_SETTING = "iki.request_side"' /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="share">
        <p>
          One check decides what a project may use: its own entities, those linked into it, and those
          whose owner allows sharing, in the same registry, that the person reads their own way, not
          through a request. Results awaiting release are left out (<Adr n={[67, 149]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={ACCESS} sym="def shareable_into(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def unreleased(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="result">
        <p>
          A result must be made in the project the request was sent to. Until it's released the
          asking project can't read it, nor the files it holds (<Adr n="0149" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={REQ} sym="def check_result(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def shareable_files(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="release">
        <p>
          Every state change calls <code>released()</code>. When the state is one the result type
          releases on, the result is linked into the asking project once. If the release was signed
          after an independent review, the reviewed revision is kept, and the result is final (<Adr
            n={[149, 179]}
          />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={REQ} sym="def released(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py" sym="requests.released(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="told">
        <p>
          <code>request.submitted</code> in the asking project, <code>request.received</code> in the
          service, and <code>request.result_released</code> back in the asking project, with the
          reviewed, signed and released revisions (<Adr n="0142" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit/log.py" sym='"request.result_released"' />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/declared.py" sym="def released(" /></p>
      </div>
    </Diagram>

    <h3 id="flow">One request, from asking to release</h3>
    <Sequence
      id="flow-seq"
      title="A Regulated QC request to a core facility"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "asker", label: "Asking project", sub: "an Editor" },
        { id: "server", label: "Server" },
        { id: "service", label: "Core facility", sub: "coordinator, analyst, QA" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "intake", from: "service", to: "server", label: "Admin: intake takes QC requests from the asker" },
        { id: "create", from: "asker", to: "server", label: "create the request, naming a tube" },
        { id: "submit", from: "asker", to: "server", label: "POST /entities/{ref}/submit" },
        { id: "link", from: "server", to: "db", label: "Request row; links into the service, via_request" },
        { id: "work", from: "service", to: "server", label: "accept, assign, in progress: service fields only" },
        { id: "deliver", from: "service", to: "server", label: "deliverable: submitted, reviewed, signed" },
        { id: "release", from: "service", to: "server", label: "QA signs Released" },
        { id: "back", from: "server", to: "db", label: "link the result back; released_review_revision" },
        { id: "read", from: "asker", to: "server", label: "GET /entities/{ref}/request: results, receipts" },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="intake"><p>Until the intake accepts the type, sending fails with the same 404 whether the service exists or not (<Adr n="0132" />).</p><p><Path p={REQ} sym="def not_a_service(" /></p></div>
      <div slot="create"><p>An unsent request is the asker's alone. Its service fields keep their defaults; the app shows it as not sent yet.</p><p><Path p={`${LIMS}/analysis_request.yaml`} /></p></div>
      <div slot="submit"><p>A request goes to one service, for good. Stale revisions get 412, as any write does.</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/requests.py" /></p></div>
      <div slot="link"><p>Only the records the request names are linked; another of the asker's tubes isn't. For a registry entry, the asker's record of it is what's sent (<Adr n="0115" />).</p><p><Path p={REQ} sym="def sent_records(" /></p></div>
      <div slot="work"><p>The server writes as the service side inside a savepoint; the database refuses anything else (<Adr n="0132" />).</p><p><Path p={REQ} sym="def writing(" /></p></div>
      <div slot="deliver"><p>The plugin's states decide who may submit, review and release, and the guards keep the reviewer from releasing what they reviewed (<Adr n={[119, 125, 128]} />).</p><p><Path p={`${LIMS}/qc_core_deliverable.yaml`} /></p></div>
      <div slot="release"><p>The signed document names the reviewed revision when the result type needs a review before release (<Adr n={[126, 179]} />).</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/services.py" sym='document["reviewed"] = reference' /></p></div>
      <div slot="back"><p>The database holds that a released-after-review result is locked, at exactly two revisions past the reviewed one (<Adr n="0179" />).</p><p><Path p={MODELS} sym='name="iki_released_review_locked"' /></p></div>
      <div slot="read"><p>The receipts are read from the <code>request.result_released</code> audit events, not a table of their own.</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/requests.py" sym="class ResultReleaseOut(" /></p></div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A training project asks the core facility to sequence tube CEY652 by long reads; the core
      answers with the public run ERR13376132. The example LIMS plugin models it, and <Path
        p="server/tests/test_core_facility.py"
        sym="def _sequencing("
      /> drives it through the services. The request type gives the core its own fields and says
      what the asker sends:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={`${LIMS}/analysis_request.yaml`} start="^request:" />
    <p>The service's status, which only the core's lab roles set:</p>
    <Excerpt file={`${LIMS}/analysis_request.yaml`} start="^  status:" />
    <p>The deliverable says which request it answers and which state releases it:</p>
    <Excerpt file={`${LIMS}/qc_core_deliverable.yaml`} start="^result:" />
    <Excerpt file={`${LIMS}/qc_core_deliverable.yaml`} start="^      released:" />
    <p>What the test establishes, in order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Before the intake takes the type, sending is refused as "no such service".</li>
      <li>After sending, the named tube is linked into the core through the request; another tube isn't.</li>
      <li>The core's deliverable and its report file answer 404 to the asker until release.</li>
      <li>A correction after review returns it to Recorded, and it's submitted and reviewed again.</li>
      <li>The reviewer can't release, and release is refused while the tube is off the request.</li>
      <li>
        After QA's release, the request's receipts name the reviewed, signed and released revisions,
        and the earlier report stays readable in the deliverable's history.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Registries, entries and project records" open>
        <p>
          A registry holds identities; an identity is registered once per registry. A project holds
          one record per entry, with its own fields, accession and HRID; a project sees an entry only
          while it holds a record of it (<Adr n={[115, 154]} />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def held_entries(" />).
          In a request, the asker sends its records, never the entry: the service reads the record
          the asker gave it. A lot is a project's record
          of an entry's material (<Adr n="0162" />). Registrars change an entry's facts through
          <code>PATCH /entries/&#123;ref&#125;</code> (<Adr n="0143" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Which side writes what">
        <p>
          The request's <code>service_fields</code> are the service's; everything else is the
          asker's. Before sending, service fields are refused with "the service's: … sets it, once
          it's submitted". A list table belongs to one side as a whole. Records named later, when the
          asker edits a sent request, go to the service too (<Adr n="0132" />, <Path
            p={REQ}
            sym="def refused_fields("
          />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def send(" />).
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={REQ} start="^ASKER, SERVICE = " />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What each project can read">
        <p>
          The service reads the request and what it sends, through links made by the request; it can't
          share those onward, because <code>shareable_into</code> counts only links the person reads
          their own way. The asker reads results only once released, and why something is held is
          one of four reasons (<Adr n={[67, 149]} />):
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={ACCESS} start="^def why_held\(" />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Release, and after it">
        <p>
          A release is told once: a result already linked back for this request isn't linked again.
          A service that has stopped sharing can't release. When the release follows an independent
          review, <code>released_review_revision</code> is set and the result is final: its values and
          states can't change, and no correction path applies (<Adr n={[149, 179]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py"
            sym="def _final_release("
          />). Before release, a correction under lock returns each state to its <code>on_edit</code>
          target with a reason (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py" sym="def return_for_edit(" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <Inferred why="Read from ResultSpec.requires_review; no test asserts it.">
            A Research flow deliverable is released by a single review signature, so no earlier
            reviewed revision is kept and its receipt has none.
          </Inferred>
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Searching a service's queue">
        <p>
          Search has a built-in <code>sent_to</code> column on the request's service, which gives a
          service its queue as an ordinary search (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/entities/search.py"
            sym='"sent_to": Column('
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0132" /> lists Withdrawn among a request's states. In the example plugin, withdrawn
            is a state of the asker's own field, <code>standing</code>, not of the service's status.
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0132" />'s database guard is broader in code: it also refuses the service's
            inserts and deletes, and any write to the other side's list tables.
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0115" />'s example looks up a record by project slug; the API takes the project's
            UUID.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <Routes tags={["requests"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={REQ} />: intake, sending, the two sides, release</li>
        <li><Path p={ACCESS} />: what each project can read and share</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/requests.py" />: the request routes</li>
        <li><Path p="web/app/src/requests.ts" />: the app's request queries</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0067" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0115" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0132" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0142" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0149" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0179" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0195" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
